Thanks for joining us, J.J.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:00.560
Absolutely.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.500
I'd love to have you
introduce yourself and share

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:06.230
a bit about your
background, and maybe

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:09.770
also let us know something you
like to do outside of work too.

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:11.300
OK.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:18.173
Well, very briefly, I obviously
do lots of software engineering

00:03:18.173 --> 00:03:19.340
and product development now.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:22.860
But when I was younger, I
studied political science.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.540
And I got very interested
in data analysis.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:29.030
But I took a deep
detour from that

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:31.430
and got into writing
software and worked on lots

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:32.930
of different software products.

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:39.493
And I think it was 11 or 12
years ago, I became aware of R.

00:03:39.493 --> 00:03:41.660
And I was actually looking
for opportunities to work

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:43.850
on open-source software.

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:45.620
And I thought it was
tremendously cool

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:48.320
that the thing that got
me interested in software

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:51.720
in the first place, and the
first thing I wanted to do,

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.910
which was data analysis,
had this really, really

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:58.560
vital open-source project
associated with it.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.040
So I said let me
get involved with R.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.630
And then I've been doing that
at RStudio and now at Posit for,

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:07.670
I think, it's been
11 or 12 years.

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:09.260
So I started the company.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:12.830
And I worked on the RStudio IDE.

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:17.329
And then I worked quite
a bit on Markdown.

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:21.829
I'm a little bit on Shiny, some
stuff related to TensorFlow.

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:25.050
And then about two
or three years ago,

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.860
I started a new
project called Quarto

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.980
which was a reimagining
of R Markdown,

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:34.550
both in terms if we
had to do it all over

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:37.700
again from the beginning,
what would we do?

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:42.920
But also to make R Markdown
multi-language to make it

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.410
so it could work for R,
and Python, and Julia,

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:51.620
and JavaScript, and really
make it available for anyone

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:53.570
who is doing scientific
communication

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:57.600
or doing data analysis of
any kind in any language.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.700
So that's what I've been
working on and continuing

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:03.140
to work on now.

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:06.530
And I got my hands in lots
of other different projects

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:09.140
here at RStudio too.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:11.300
And then, what do I like
to do outside of work?

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:15.020
I like to play
Magic the Gathering.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:16.040
Awesome.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:17.940
Thank you.

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:21.290
So I'd love to
ask you, what made

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:26.450
you so interested in
open source initially?

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:31.220
It was really that
the first couple

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:34.340
big software projects I worked
on were proprietary software.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:40.700
And I found it disappointing
that proprietary software

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:45.770
products get very tied up with
the company that sponsors them.

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:50.060
And the fate of that company
and the other products

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:54.440
that company might offer, or who
that company gets acquired by,

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:57.830
really end up affecting
the fate of the projects.

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:01.390
And I also found that
some proprietary software

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:04.140
is either cheap or
free or low cost,

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:09.140
but there's a certain impediment
to adoption associated

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:13.010
with the price, that
as a creative person

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:16.700
I want my work to be available
as broadly as possible.

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:22.130
So both, it's make the work
available to as many people as

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:24.350
possible, but also
there's a dimension

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.330
of the durability of the work.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:30.118
Is the work going to be around
in 30 years or 40 years?

00:06:30.118 --> 00:06:31.910
What are the things
that would make it not?

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:36.600
So that that's what made
open source appealing to me.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.170
I'm seeing a lot of love for
Quarto in the chat right now.

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:42.450
But while we wait just a
minute here for questions

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:44.880
to come in from
everybody, I'm also

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:46.860
curious, what are
you most excited

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:49.910
about at Posit in
the next year ahead?

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:51.060
The next year?

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.580
Well, it's going to be
related to Quarto to,

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:58.200
of course, because that's what
I'm spending all my time on.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:02.700
I would say that the thing
I'm excited about is--

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:05.460
and you can see
some of this work

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:09.030
in the visual editor
that's in RStudio.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:15.930
But we recognize that Quarto is
really a powerful and flexible

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:20.370
and actually easy to use
for certain subset of users

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:22.380
who are very technical
and very motivated.

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:25.720
But we actually want to make
Quarto available more broadly.

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:30.390
And so working on tooling
that lets technical people

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:33.570
collaborate with non-technical
people over documents,

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:36.900
and also lets some less
technical people participate

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:41.850
in using Quarto, are
the things that I'm

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:43.570
focused on for the next year.

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:48.710
And I'm excited to see
those getting realized.

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:49.210
Awesome.

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:51.370
Sam, I see you put a
question to the chat.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:53.210
Do you want to jump in?

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:55.690
Yeah, I just
wanted to see what

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:58.780
your thoughts were on AI
tools like ChatGPT and GitHub

00:07:58.780 --> 00:07:59.300
Copilot.

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:07.940
Yeah, I think that I'm a
little concerned that companies

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:11.150
are rushing these things out
in the sort of excitement

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:13.790
and novelty of them
and have not thought

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:20.810
through the implications
of their broader use.

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:25.850
Something like ChatGPT
is not really like--

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:29.840
the creators of it don't
have full control of it.

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:32.570
You can see that
in the safeguards

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:34.669
that they attempted to
put in, but then you

00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:36.380
can circumvent the
safeguards and get

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.780
it to tell you things that
it's not supposed to tell you.

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:43.760
So I think things that
are happening in AI,

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:46.940
and generative AI
specifically, are exciting.

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:53.870
But I feel technology companies
and technologists sometimes

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:55.880
feel like, we should
do this because we can,

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:58.970
or we should do this because
it's cool, and haven't thought

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:03.380
about the broader social,
political, economic

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:04.440
implications.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:10.160
So I feel certainly exciting
to see us come up with AI

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:12.680
that is that
powerful, but I think

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:14.150
we're going a little too fast.

00:09:14.150 --> 00:09:17.410
That would be my position.

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:19.710
Thank you.

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:22.170
Libby, I saw you also asked
a question in the chat.

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:24.690
You want to jump in?

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:25.410
Sure.

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:27.210
I was wondering,
because I've been

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:30.420
doing a lot of
retrospection lately,

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:33.450
in hindsight, what is
one of the best decisions

00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:38.410
that you ever made for
your career your education?

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:43.730
I would say the best
decision that I ever

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:46.190
made is probably that--

00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:48.860
and this, actually, has
a lot of commonality

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:51.120
with talking to a lot of
people about their careers.

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:54.200
I think when I was
about in my late 30s,

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:57.530
I had been involved in
both developing software

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.510
and also starting companies.

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:02.150
And I really
learned about myself

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:07.100
that the company part
of things, management

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:11.630
and the entrepreneurship, I
really did not like at all.

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:12.840
I did not like it.

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:13.800
I didn't enjoy it.

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:14.370
I didn't.

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:20.090
And so I said, I'm happy to be
involved in starting companies,

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:22.650
but I'm absolutely not
going to do that part.

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:26.420
And I'm going to have to find a
partner or other people who are

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:27.800
excited about doing that part.

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:30.380
And what I really want to
do is focus on engineering

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:31.620
and product development.

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:34.700
And it's very easy
as a company founder

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:37.800
to get pulled into all
kinds of other things.

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:40.340
And that's what happened
to me the first two times.

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:43.070
And just being super clear
about that and saying,

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:47.150
I won't even do it, unless I
can satisfy this condition.

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:52.220
So I think that's pretty broadly
applicable in that a lot of us

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:54.740
accumulate a lot of
responsibility in our careers.

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:56.990
And some of it is
necessary and important

00:10:56.990 --> 00:10:58.910
for a given role or company.

00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:01.820
But then, eventually,
being clear about what do

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:04.340
I really like to do
and really want to do,

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:07.490
and what do I feel like I should
do or what is put upon me.

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:11.690
I know a lot of people now,
who are in their 40s, who

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:15.625
have actually managed dozens
of people and are like, yeah,

00:11:15.625 --> 00:11:17.250
I don't really want
to do that anymore.

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:19.200
I don't ever want to have
anyone work for me anymore.

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:20.630
So I'm going to be
really clear about that.

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:21.770
And I'm going to walk in.

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:23.570
And people are going to
try to give me people.

00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:25.220
They're going to try
to make me a manager.

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:26.370
And I'm not going to do it.

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:28.610
So I think it applies
pretty broadly.

00:11:28.610 --> 00:11:33.440
But just knowing yourself
and setting pretty rigid

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:37.550
constraints about what you're
willing to do in the workplace

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:40.270
and not.

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:40.870
Thank you.

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:42.970
That's awesome.

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:43.480
Thank you.

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:44.897
That's something
that's definitely

00:11:44.897 --> 00:11:48.910
come up quite a few times in
this space too, in that jump

00:11:48.910 --> 00:11:52.290
to management and then you
lose out on the technical side.

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:53.290
Yeah, it's a hard one.

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:54.373
And everybody's different.

00:11:54.373 --> 00:11:56.810
And both can be
really rewarding.

00:11:56.810 --> 00:12:00.700
I know a lot of people who
find management very rewarding.

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:05.530
But I know a lot of people
who find it really alienating.

00:12:05.530 --> 00:12:08.592
I see a few anonymous
questions that came in too,

00:12:08.592 --> 00:12:10.300
so I want to make sure
I jump over there.

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:13.540
And one was, will Posit
work to make Python

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:16.870
as enjoyable to use as R?

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:20.230
Well, we have a different,
special relationship with R

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:22.330
in that we have the tidyverse.

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:24.460
We've defined this
sort of system

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:28.150
within the system that's
totally internally, hopefully,

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:31.060
as much as possible, totally
internally consistent

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:33.847
and is really well
tooled end to end.

00:12:33.847 --> 00:12:35.680
I don't know that we'll
ever be able to have

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:39.010
that relationship to
Python because Python,

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:40.930
they're just so vast.

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:43.520
And people are piecing together
so many different things

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:45.320
from so many different places.

00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:47.973
So I think we'll
make contributions.

00:12:47.973 --> 00:12:49.390
We're definitely
focused right now

00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:52.150
on doing Quarto for Python
and Shiny for Python.

00:12:54.760 --> 00:12:58.870
I don't actually think that
any company is big enough

00:12:58.870 --> 00:13:03.370
to do for Python what
we sort of did for R.

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:08.260
So I think we'll make it
more pleasurable in places.

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:09.970
But I don't I don't
know that it's

00:13:09.970 --> 00:13:16.640
going to be anything like
what we've done with R.

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:20.240
Yu, I see you put a question
into the chat a bit earlier.

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:21.900
Do you want to jump in next?

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:22.400
Sure.

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:25.280
Hi, J.J. Actually my
question is kind of piggyback

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:26.360
on what you just said.

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:30.320
So R as a programming language
is almost 30 years old.

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:33.170
And R versus Python
is an eternal topic.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:38.090
So where do you see R
in the next ten years?

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:41.300
So actually, R is
even over 40 years

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:46.520
old, if you count S,
which is its precursor.

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:50.900
I think what we're
trying to do with R,

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:54.200
and you can see a lot of
the stuff that we've done,

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:56.570
and maybe it's not
all super visible yet,

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:58.130
but some of the
things we've done

00:13:58.130 --> 00:14:00.260
in the tidyverse
in the last year

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:05.670
have been focused on making
error messages much better.

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:09.650
And so I feel R
has the potential

00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:17.410
to go over the transom from
people who do not program,

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:19.160
do not think of
themselves as programmers,

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:22.760
have not programmed,
to do successful going

00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:24.950
to code first data science.

00:14:24.950 --> 00:14:28.320
I think R is actually pretty
uniquely positioned to do that.

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:30.960
And I feel we are just
getting started with that.

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:40.460
So the people today, they may
use Excel or SPSS or Tableau.

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:43.160
They're doing data analysis,
but they're actually not

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:46.730
able to ask and answer all
the questions they should.

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:51.590
I see R fitting right
in that spot of the best

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:54.680
way for someone who thinks
themself, I'm a biologist,

00:14:54.680 --> 00:14:58.010
I'm an economist, but I need
to answer difficult questions

00:14:58.010 --> 00:14:59.310
about data.

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:02.000
So I see R getting better
and better and better

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:03.200
in that vector.

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:05.210
And that's certainly
where we're putting

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:08.210
our time at least in the
tidyverse team right now.

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:14.220
So we're now getting
confessions into Slido too.

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:17.570
Someone said, so this
is a confession and also

00:15:17.570 --> 00:15:19.160
a call for suggestions.

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:22.130
I love dplyr Shiny,
and R, but I'm

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:24.470
switching to Python
simply because it's

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:27.980
listed in a lot more jobs and
gives a better starting point

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:31.470
for a possible career change
in other areas of programming.

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:36.520
So what would you advise
for someone in my position?

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:41.140
Well, there are a lot of ideas
in R that you're familiar with.

00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:42.840
And then some of
those are carried over

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:46.020
to different
libraries in Python.

00:15:46.020 --> 00:15:49.680
So you can try to find
the places in Python

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:52.020
and adopt the projects.

00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:54.180
There's definitely some of the--

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:59.070
I think Plot 9, and I
think it's, like, "Sluba,"

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:02.340
sort of a dplyr-like library.

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:05.640
So there are Python libraries
that are very much inspired

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:07.920
by the R counterparts.

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:08.850
You can adopt those.

00:16:08.850 --> 00:16:11.550
You can get involved with those.

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:13.770
I don't think you're
going to have quite

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:16.110
the same end-to-end experience.

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:22.800
But you can, like I said, get
involved with the projects

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:30.030
that you do in Python, what
you loved so much about R,

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:34.430
and try to get those
more widely adopted.

00:16:34.430 --> 00:16:35.160
Thank you.

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:35.660
I see.

00:16:35.660 --> 00:16:37.077
Thanks, Jake, for
sharing the link

00:16:37.077 --> 00:16:40.760
