00:18:40.615 --> 00:18:41.990
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
Congratulations.

00:18:41.990 --> 00:18:45.170
--changing diapers
and everything, whatnot.

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:48.380
So I work for the
Panama Canal Authority.

00:18:48.380 --> 00:18:52.220
And the Panama
Canal has recently

00:18:52.220 --> 00:18:55.340
created a data-science unit.

00:18:55.340 --> 00:18:59.750
And so basically,
we're 16 people there.

00:18:59.750 --> 00:19:02.690
And I'm the only one
that is a heavy R

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:04.620
user because we're
divided, these like me

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:05.870
against the rest of the world.

00:19:05.870 --> 00:19:07.970
It's not like [INAUDIBLE].

00:19:07.970 --> 00:19:09.990
So we work in many
different projects.

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:12.900
And basically,
everybody else does

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:14.900
everything, like
machine-learning, and that sort

00:19:14.900 --> 00:19:16.250
of thing, in Python.

00:19:16.250 --> 00:19:23.030
And then I do my thing in
R. So my question would be,

00:19:23.030 --> 00:19:24.020
how much--

00:19:24.020 --> 00:19:26.570
I mean, there's always a debate.

00:19:26.570 --> 00:19:28.970
And you always find
on the internet

00:19:28.970 --> 00:19:31.880
these papers and
discussions, whether to use

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:34.160
R or Python for
machine-learning, for AI,

00:19:34.160 --> 00:19:35.630
or whatever.

00:19:35.630 --> 00:19:40.700
And I've seen all sorts
of opinions about it.

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:42.680
There's people
that it's like, OK,

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:47.810
Python is better when it comes
to AI and machine-learning.

00:19:47.810 --> 00:19:51.020
And then R is better for
statistical computing

00:19:51.020 --> 00:19:54.560
or for computational
statistics purposes.

00:19:54.560 --> 00:19:55.730
Now, I'm an actuary.

00:19:55.730 --> 00:20:00.290
And I've been using R since I
worked for insurance companies

00:20:00.290 --> 00:20:02.570
in maybe 2006.

00:20:02.570 --> 00:20:07.540
And this is probably
a testimonial also.

00:20:07.540 --> 00:20:12.500
I've never had the
need to use Python.

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:17.510
I've used R for almost
all my life for doing

00:20:17.510 --> 00:20:19.700
computational stats
and data analysis

00:20:19.700 --> 00:20:22.530
and for [INAUDIBLE]
series and whatnot.

00:20:22.530 --> 00:20:27.560
So my question would be,
in terms of its capability

00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:30.500
of dealing with large
data sets, in terms

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:37.880
of its capability for modeling,
machine-learning purposes

00:20:37.880 --> 00:20:42.380
and everything, how would you
say that R compares to Python?

00:20:42.380 --> 00:20:44.090
Because that's
something I'm always

00:20:44.090 --> 00:20:49.550
interested in knowing
because I'm a heavy R user.

00:20:49.550 --> 00:20:50.900
And I'm not saying that Python--

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:55.052
I mean, Python, both are
wonderful tools for data

00:20:55.052 --> 00:20:56.010
science and everything.

00:20:56.010 --> 00:21:02.720
But to me, I'm at Justin Hopkins
University getting a master's

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:04.040
degree in data science.

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:07.500
And I try to use R
as much as I can.

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:10.640
I've been exposed to
Python, but still I

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:14.390
feel that statistical
analysis I think

00:21:14.390 --> 00:21:17.468
is way easier with
R than with Python.

00:21:17.468 --> 00:21:18.260
Yeah, definitely.

00:21:18.260 --> 00:21:21.770
I would say statistical
analysis, interactive data

00:21:21.770 --> 00:21:27.590
exploration, communicate,
creating data products that

00:21:27.590 --> 00:21:30.387
communicate things, R
is better than Python

00:21:30.387 --> 00:21:31.595
in all those things, I think.

00:21:34.250 --> 00:21:36.950
It's not as widely
used as a language.

00:21:36.950 --> 00:21:39.350
So it's not as general purpose.

00:21:39.350 --> 00:21:42.860
And in terms of
large data sets, I

00:21:42.860 --> 00:21:50.180
think mostly that is handled
by either external systems you

00:21:50.180 --> 00:21:53.960
interface with or native
codes, or Dplyr or DataTable

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:56.570
or DuckDB or Arrow or Spark.

00:21:56.570 --> 00:21:59.660
There's some means
by which you're

00:21:59.660 --> 00:22:01.400
able to access large data.

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:04.470
It's not really confined
by the language.

00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:08.630
And I think that our interfaces
to those technologies

00:22:08.630 --> 00:22:10.040
are pretty comparable to Python.

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:12.420
I do think, in terms
of machine-learning,

00:22:12.420 --> 00:22:15.110
there's a lot of
machine-learning packages in R.

00:22:15.110 --> 00:22:19.850
And some of the most important
things, like XGBoost and stuff,

00:22:19.850 --> 00:22:24.620
work very well in
R. We have created

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:30.410
deep-learning interfaces in R
both to TensorFlow and Torch.

00:22:30.410 --> 00:22:33.110
But Python probably does have
an edge in deep-learning,

00:22:33.110 --> 00:22:36.650
I would say, in that you
can use those libraries.

00:22:36.650 --> 00:22:39.650
And there's probably
more extensions

00:22:39.650 --> 00:22:42.620
to those things
that are available.

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:49.430
But deep-learning is one
part of machine-learning,

00:22:49.430 --> 00:22:51.420
one part of working with data.

00:22:51.420 --> 00:22:52.820
And it's not really predominant.

00:22:52.820 --> 00:22:55.940
In some fields it's
really quite important,

00:22:55.940 --> 00:22:57.710
but it's not really predominant.

00:22:57.710 --> 00:23:05.840
So I think what we're
hoping to make easy

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:09.740
for our users is a world
where if you want to use R,

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:12.230
and it is so much easier
for so many things,

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:14.720
it's very easy for
you to make that case,

00:23:14.720 --> 00:23:17.450
things like articulate
for interoperability,

00:23:17.450 --> 00:23:18.380
things like that.

00:23:18.380 --> 00:23:22.290
We're trying to make it
easy to make that case.

00:23:22.290 --> 00:23:26.680
And so I would say like for
the things you're doing,

00:23:26.680 --> 00:23:29.230
R is clearly better.

00:23:29.230 --> 00:23:32.500
I could certainly see
where if someone says,

00:23:32.500 --> 00:23:36.500
I'm using PyTorch, I like the
Python interface to PyTorch

00:23:36.500 --> 00:23:37.000
better.

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:37.500
Sure.

