shout out that you're going
to give, which is awesome.

00:27:56.650 --> 00:27:57.730
I always love to
hear more about that.

00:27:57.730 --> 00:27:59.272
But just kind of
key pieces of advice

00:27:59.272 --> 00:28:02.000
for talking to CEOs,
talking to business leaders,

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:05.070
orgs, and convincing
at a high level

00:28:05.070 --> 00:28:07.320
that this is the direction
that we should go and still

00:28:07.320 --> 00:28:09.090
be sustainable as a business.

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:16.658
Being just a
technology vendor first,

00:28:16.658 --> 00:28:19.200
it's a little different, because
you guys aren't a technology

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:20.070
vendor first.

00:28:20.070 --> 00:28:23.232
I'm assuming you're work
in a pharma company.

00:28:23.232 --> 00:28:24.690
And so you're trying
to figure out,

00:28:24.690 --> 00:28:27.810
how can we create more
ground for collaboration

00:28:27.810 --> 00:28:30.875
with other pharma companies
and the community?

00:28:30.875 --> 00:28:31.500
That's right.

00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:34.260
And I think there
you might make the case

00:28:34.260 --> 00:28:39.600
that these sorts of
things enable everyone

00:28:39.600 --> 00:28:41.130
to collectively move forward.

00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:46.590
And then our core
competency is like--

00:28:46.590 --> 00:28:49.380
we always think of
RStudio as the things

00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:52.710
that are paid products or
things that you buy when you're

00:28:52.710 --> 00:28:55.350
trying to adopt R at scale.

00:28:55.350 --> 00:28:58.183
If you're an
individual, you rarely

00:28:58.183 --> 00:28:59.850
would have need for
any of these things.

00:28:59.850 --> 00:29:02.017
But it's like, oh, we're
an organization of hundreds

00:29:02.017 --> 00:29:04.290
of people or dozens
of people, or we're

00:29:04.290 --> 00:29:06.265
trying to deploy things
on the web securely,

00:29:06.265 --> 00:29:07.390
and all that sort of thing.

00:29:07.390 --> 00:29:10.230
So I think there's
the line there.

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:12.870
It's sort of at scale.

00:29:12.870 --> 00:29:16.590
And so it's not exactly
the same reasoning chain.

00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:19.890
But it is some kind of a
virtuous cycle thing, where

00:29:19.890 --> 00:29:22.710
I think you need to make
the case that somehow

00:29:22.710 --> 00:29:28.500
more collaboration with the
community and other companies

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:32.760
will create situations where
your core competency will

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:34.830
be more valuable.

00:29:34.830 --> 00:29:37.560
I know that was one of
the rationales for Google,

00:29:37.560 --> 00:29:39.690
when they open-sourced
TensorFlow.

00:29:39.690 --> 00:29:42.930
They basically said, well,
were going to be really, really

00:29:42.930 --> 00:29:46.920
good at training
TensorFlow models

00:29:46.920 --> 00:29:48.370
and hosting TensorFlow models.

00:29:48.370 --> 00:29:51.690
So if we get lots of
people using TensorFlow,

00:29:51.690 --> 00:29:53.370
then that's good for us.

00:29:53.370 --> 00:29:55.950
Or we get lots of
machine-learning researchers

00:29:55.950 --> 00:29:58.260
using TensorFlow, then when
they come to work for us,

00:29:58.260 --> 00:29:59.730
they already know TensorFlow.

00:29:59.730 --> 00:30:02.820
And so I think there can be
things like that, where you're

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:04.500
creating a broader community.

00:30:04.500 --> 00:30:06.060
You're creating network effects.

00:30:06.060 --> 00:30:09.330
And your company then
is in a good position

00:30:09.330 --> 00:30:14.241
to help people use
those things at scale.

00:30:14.241 --> 00:30:17.430
I can think of like
Databricks has a thing called

00:30:17.430 --> 00:30:19.698
ML Flow, which is like a
machine-learning experiment

00:30:19.698 --> 00:30:20.490
management library.

00:30:20.490 --> 00:30:21.230
I think their rationale--

00:30:21.230 --> 00:30:22.380
I think they were
very clear about it.

00:30:22.380 --> 00:30:23.980
They're like, this
is open source.

00:30:23.980 --> 00:30:25.230
You can run it anywhere.

00:30:25.230 --> 00:30:27.720
Anybody can run it on their
own machine, whatever.

00:30:27.720 --> 00:30:30.750
But we think we're going to
be really good at hosting

00:30:30.750 --> 00:30:32.850
machine-learning
workloads at Databricks,

00:30:32.850 --> 00:30:35.010
so we think if more
people use this,

00:30:35.010 --> 00:30:37.590
it's going to be easier for
them to eventually interact

00:30:37.590 --> 00:30:38.700
with Databricks.

00:30:38.700 --> 00:30:42.090
So there may be rationale
along those lines

00:30:42.090 --> 00:30:44.195
in your particular industry.

00:30:44.195 --> 00:30:45.320
Those are great examples.

00:30:45.320 --> 00:30:45.960
Thanks so much.

00:30:45.960 --> 00:30:47.040
Sure.

00:30:47.040 --> 00:30:48.450
Thank you.

00:30:48.450 --> 00:30:52.200
And, Lisa, I wanted to
add on to your question

00:30:52.200 --> 00:30:54.870
too and just share
a helpful resource.

00:30:54.870 --> 00:30:57.540
Sorry, sometimes it's hard
for me to hear the question

00:30:57.540 --> 00:31:02.070
and look at the questions in
the chat at the same time.

00:31:02.070 --> 00:31:06.900
But Tiger Tang, who
works at Carfax,

00:31:06.900 --> 00:31:11.160
gave a great talk at
the RStudio conference

00:31:11.160 --> 00:31:15.030
this year on saving
1,000 hours with RStudio.

00:31:15.030 --> 00:31:17.220
And it was selling
R in your workplace.

00:31:17.220 --> 00:31:19.500
But a big lesson
there was, like,

00:31:19.500 --> 00:31:23.940
selling the power of automation
and a lot of the time saved.

00:31:23.940 --> 00:31:27.465
So I would definitely recommend
checking out that talk too.

00:31:31.050 --> 00:31:35.510
So jumping over to some of
the other questions here.

00:31:38.280 --> 00:31:40.590
Let me jump up to
some from earlier.

00:31:40.590 --> 00:31:45.910
One on Slido, J.J., was, how
does your usual day at work

00:31:45.910 --> 00:31:46.410
look?

00:31:46.410 --> 00:31:49.860
Any suggestions
for a middle-aged,

00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:55.225
new-to-data-science career
trying to balance work

00:31:55.225 --> 00:31:55.725
and life?

00:32:00.570 --> 00:32:05.490
One of the things I try to
do is strictly segment my time,

00:32:05.490 --> 00:32:06.960
so I have time
that I can just be

00:32:06.960 --> 00:32:10.020
absorbed in solving
problems or writing

00:32:10.020 --> 00:32:11.680
code or things like that.

00:32:11.680 --> 00:32:17.970
And that time, I refuse to be
interrupted during that time.

00:32:17.970 --> 00:32:20.880
I don't interleave
those kind of things

00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:24.090
that require more concentration
and thinking with things

00:32:24.090 --> 00:32:25.750
that are more transactional.

00:32:25.750 --> 00:32:28.320
And so I might come to work.

00:32:28.320 --> 00:32:31.620
And I might spend
four or five hours

00:32:31.620 --> 00:32:34.450
working on various problems
that are in front of me.

00:32:34.450 --> 00:32:36.870
And then I might
spend 45 minutes

00:32:36.870 --> 00:32:39.390
looking at my email and
slack and just seeing,

00:32:39.390 --> 00:32:41.640
has anyone been
trying to communicate

00:32:41.640 --> 00:32:42.540
with me this morning?

00:32:42.540 --> 00:32:44.370
How urgent are those things?

00:32:44.370 --> 00:32:46.740
And then, similarly, I
might do the same thing

00:32:46.740 --> 00:32:47.670
later on in the day.

00:32:47.670 --> 00:32:49.670
I might say, OK, now I'm
going to do that again.

00:32:49.670 --> 00:32:53.490
But I think not allowing
yourself to be interrupted

00:32:53.490 --> 00:32:55.710
is really important.

00:32:55.710 --> 00:33:00.450
And even if you're
currently going from a place

00:33:00.450 --> 00:33:03.300
where you're interrupted
the whole day long

00:33:03.300 --> 00:33:05.340
and you're constantly
trying to interleave

00:33:05.340 --> 00:33:10.740
high-concentration things,
you might be getting like way

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:12.190
less done than you could.

00:33:12.190 --> 00:33:14.580
And so even if you
say, well, my job

00:33:14.580 --> 00:33:17.200
actually requires quite
a bit of interaction,

00:33:17.200 --> 00:33:18.570
even if you could set aside--

00:33:18.570 --> 00:33:20.700
I'm trying to set aside
six or seven hours

00:33:20.700 --> 00:33:21.900
for a deep work every day.

00:33:21.900 --> 00:33:23.400
If you could set
aside two or three,

00:33:23.400 --> 00:33:25.567
or four or five, whatever
it is that you can manage,

00:33:25.567 --> 00:33:29.070
that makes a big difference.

00:33:29.070 --> 00:33:33.420
And I also try to be pretty--

00:33:33.420 --> 00:33:37.200
There's lots of things that
you could do in your work

00:33:37.200 --> 00:33:38.490
and in your job.

00:33:38.490 --> 00:33:42.150
There's lots of things
that would be a good idea.

00:33:42.150 --> 00:33:45.240
And I try to be really
choosy about what I actually

00:33:45.240 --> 00:33:47.070
say, yes, we're going to do.

00:33:47.070 --> 00:33:49.380
Because I feel if I say
yes to too many things,

00:33:49.380 --> 00:33:52.115
then it's very
dilutive to the things

00:33:52.115 --> 00:33:53.240
that really, really matter.

00:33:56.530 --> 00:33:59.770
How do you find six to
seven hours of focus time

00:33:59.770 --> 00:34:02.080
as the CEO with
all the meetings?

00:34:02.080 --> 00:34:05.500
This is what I said earlier.

00:34:05.500 --> 00:34:08.260
Maybe some of you may
have met Tareef Kawaf,

00:34:08.260 --> 00:34:10.630
who's our president of RStudio.

00:34:10.630 --> 00:34:13.659
He's been here for 10 years.

00:34:13.659 --> 00:34:15.139
And that was our deal.

00:34:15.139 --> 00:34:18.780
I said, I need to code
for seven hours a day.

00:34:18.780 --> 00:34:21.659
So you got me for
as much as you need.

00:34:21.659 --> 00:34:22.670
I'm your partner.

00:34:22.670 --> 00:34:25.489
We can work whatever
issues we need to work.

00:34:25.489 --> 00:34:26.420
I'm always here.

00:34:26.420 --> 00:34:29.749
But it can't be a world
where I'm getting--

00:34:32.300 --> 00:34:34.219
seven is like a
rough approximation,

00:34:34.219 --> 00:34:36.320
but on any given
day, it might vary.

00:34:36.320 --> 00:34:39.530
But I think it goes
back to being clear

00:34:39.530 --> 00:34:47.030
like, OK, someone else needs
to be tending to those things.

00:34:47.030 --> 00:34:48.230
Thank you.

00:34:48.230 --> 00:34:52.580
Michael, I see you had asked
a question much earlier

00:34:52.580 --> 00:34:54.739
about the company roadmap.

00:34:54.739 --> 00:34:55.820
Do you want to jump in?

00:34:58.310 --> 00:34:58.810
Sure.

00:34:58.810 --> 00:35:00.050
I can jump in.

00:35:00.050 --> 00:35:01.480
Hi, J.J..

00:35:01.480 --> 00:35:03.970
I feel like I'm
talking to a celebrity

00:35:03.970 --> 00:35:05.830
having been such an
RStudio fan, so I

00:35:05.830 --> 00:35:09.800
appreciate you hopping on the
last call of the year here.

00:35:09.800 --> 00:35:14.590
I was just curious, being such
a big fan of the R community,

00:35:14.590 --> 00:35:18.310
what the roadmap looks like for
Posit, and maybe one-, three-,

00:35:18.310 --> 00:35:22.450
five-year strategy
quick high level.

00:35:22.450 --> 00:35:26.240
Well, I think on
the open source side,

00:35:26.240 --> 00:35:33.130
you can see that we are trying
to make Quarto and Shiny

00:35:33.130 --> 00:35:36.280
multi-language things.

00:35:36.280 --> 00:35:46.280
On the modeling side, we have
some Python-oriented modeling

00:35:46.280 --> 00:35:47.210
projects.

00:35:47.210 --> 00:35:49.160
And we have tidy models.

00:35:49.160 --> 00:35:54.440
So it's like TBD
how deep we're going

00:35:54.440 --> 00:35:56.450
to go in terms of
Python-modeling,

00:35:56.450 --> 00:36:01.490
but we certainly have a big
commitment to tidy models.

00:36:01.490 --> 00:36:13.360
On the commercial product side,
our products now, I think,

00:36:13.360 --> 00:36:17.390
they don't work together quite
as well as we'd like them to.

00:36:17.390 --> 00:36:23.600
So I think kind of putting
the tea in Posit team,

00:36:23.600 --> 00:36:28.970
making those products connect
in Workbench, and Desktop

00:36:28.970 --> 00:36:31.490
and PackageManager all work
together really, really

00:36:31.490 --> 00:36:33.890
well is important to us.

00:36:33.890 --> 00:36:36.110
Integrating those things
with the other things

00:36:36.110 --> 00:36:38.630
that people are doing in their
enterprise with data science

00:36:38.630 --> 00:36:41.420
is really important to us.

00:36:41.420 --> 00:36:44.750
Providing a cloud
version of those things

00:36:44.750 --> 00:36:47.390
is really important
as well, letting

00:36:47.390 --> 00:36:49.490
people maybe start using
the products in the cloud

00:36:49.490 --> 00:36:52.430
and then move to on-prem
or VPC-based things,

00:36:52.430 --> 00:36:56.840
or vise versa, just getting
the breadth of products

00:36:56.840 --> 00:37:01.010
scaled to what the customer
is able to adopt and absorb.

00:37:01.010 --> 00:37:02.750
And then I think
we actually see,

00:37:02.750 --> 00:37:05.900
this as a newer thing,
a significant role

00:37:05.900 --> 00:37:10.310
for our student academy in
providing learning experiences

00:37:10.310 --> 00:37:18.720
that are really not superficial
and transactional, but really

00:37:18.720 --> 00:37:20.010
deep.

00:37:20.010 --> 00:37:23.070
And so we're pretty excited
about what we've done

00:37:23.070 --> 00:37:24.930
with RStudio Academy so far.

00:37:24.930 --> 00:37:26.860
And we'll be doing a
lot more with that.

00:37:26.860 --> 00:37:32.220
So that's some of the
stuff that's on our mind.

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:33.060
Thank you.

00:37:33.060 --> 00:37:35.000
Sure.

00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:38.150
Eric, I see you just put a
question into the chat as well.

00:37:38.150 --> 00:37:39.740
Do you want to jump in next?

00:37:39.740 --> 00:37:40.430
Yes.

00:37:40.430 --> 00:37:41.290
Hi, J.J..

00:37:41.290 --> 00:37:45.170
I apologize for my voice,
getting over a nasty flu thing.

00:37:45.170 --> 00:37:48.170
But while my voice
works here, I was

00:37:48.170 --> 00:37:52.850
curious how you and your
various teams at Posit,

00:37:52.850 --> 00:37:56.300
when you think about launching a
big initiative such as Corridor

00:37:56.300 --> 00:38:00.680
or Shiny for Python, et cetera,
what are you using internally

00:38:00.680 --> 00:38:04.100
or, what you can reveal, to
really quantify if that's

00:38:04.100 --> 00:38:06.350
really being successful or not?

00:38:06.350 --> 00:38:10.220
Because a lot of times when
I have ideas at the day job,

00:38:10.220 --> 00:38:12.950
where I think we can
transform the way we operate

00:38:12.950 --> 00:38:15.980
or the way we analyze
results, it always

00:38:15.980 --> 00:38:19.670
comes back to leadership
teams saying, well, what's

00:38:19.670 --> 00:38:21.350
the value you're giving us.

00:38:21.350 --> 00:38:24.230
And that's a hard question
for me to answer sometimes.

00:38:24.230 --> 00:38:25.730
I wonder how you approach those.

00:38:25.730 --> 00:38:27.950
Well, I look at
those things like--

00:38:27.950 --> 00:38:30.260
I always look at usage metrics.

00:38:30.260 --> 00:38:33.080
And there's different
proxies for usage metrics.

00:38:33.080 --> 00:38:37.040
Package downloads is one usage
metric that you have often.

00:38:37.040 --> 00:38:42.320
And so I look at what's
the trend of usage?

00:38:42.320 --> 00:38:44.450
Is it an accelerating trend?

00:38:44.450 --> 00:38:45.740
So I don't know.

00:38:45.740 --> 00:38:50.553
In absolute terms, if there's a
million downloads of the Shiny

00:38:50.553 --> 00:38:52.970
package, I don't know if that's
a lot or little, actually,

00:38:52.970 --> 00:38:55.610
because I don't know if most
people download it eight times

00:38:55.610 --> 00:38:57.390
or--

00:38:57.390 --> 00:38:59.990
So I look at
momentum of projects

00:38:59.990 --> 00:39:03.110
and I see what the trend
line looks like over time

00:39:03.110 --> 00:39:05.300
and, again, is it accelerating?

00:39:05.300 --> 00:39:09.230
As a way of gauging, are we
having the impact that we want?

00:39:09.230 --> 00:39:11.840
Or is it growing organically?

00:39:11.840 --> 00:39:14.460
Is word of mouth leading
to more adoption?

00:39:14.460 --> 00:39:18.680
So when I see projects that
are flat in their adoption,

00:39:18.680 --> 00:39:21.920
I'm questioning, is that as
important as we thought it was?

00:39:21.920 --> 00:39:26.990
Because if it's flat, it
means natural word of mouth

00:39:26.990 --> 00:39:28.580
isn't growing it.

00:39:28.580 --> 00:39:31.250
So that's, I think,
the best we can really

00:39:31.250 --> 00:39:34.910
do is just look at
like adoption metrics

00:39:34.910 --> 00:39:38.012
and how they grow over time.

00:39:38.012 --> 00:39:39.420
Thank you.

00:39:39.420 --> 00:39:41.220
And comparative
adoption metrics.

00:39:41.220 --> 00:39:45.240
You go like, what are some
analogs to what we're doing?

00:39:45.240 --> 00:39:47.430
OK, well, how do our
adoption metrics look like

00:39:47.430 --> 00:39:50.250
compared to that analog?

00:39:50.250 --> 00:39:54.300
Yeah, I do want to mention, at
least for having Posit connect,

00:39:54.300 --> 00:39:57.270
the API does give us some
interesting metrics on, say,

00:39:57.270 --> 00:39:59.145
Shiny app usage and
things like that.

00:39:59.145 --> 00:40:00.270
So that's at least a start.

00:40:00.270 --> 00:40:02.120
Yeah, and that's a
great thing to say.

00:40:02.120 --> 00:40:04.890
Hey, look, we started
with this many users.

00:40:04.890 --> 00:40:08.010
Look, it's like tripled.

00:40:08.010 --> 00:40:11.710
So that's the most convincing
way to make the case, I think.

00:40:11.710 --> 00:40:12.210
Great.

00:40:12.210 --> 00:40:13.110
Thank you very much.

00:40:16.130 --> 00:40:18.980
I see somebody else
had asked on Slido,

00:40:18.980 --> 00:40:22.400
any advice on getting involved
with Posit's open-source

00:40:22.400 --> 00:40:25.880
efforts, specifically in the
Shiny or machine-learning

00:40:25.880 --> 00:40:26.660
areas?

00:40:26.660 --> 00:40:27.950
Yep.

00:40:27.950 --> 00:40:30.410
I mean, I'll tell
you we always welcome

00:40:30.410 --> 00:40:33.020
people who get involved.

00:40:33.020 --> 00:40:34.670
And in many cases,
we've ended up

00:40:34.670 --> 00:40:37.310
hiring people who get really,
really involved in projects.

00:40:37.310 --> 00:40:39.830
And then the first
way to get involved,

00:40:39.830 --> 00:40:43.970
if you're just like, I'm
not comfortable making pull

00:40:43.970 --> 00:40:46.610
requests or really trying
to understand the code,

00:40:46.610 --> 00:40:50.840
is just like reporting
issues and making

00:40:50.840 --> 00:40:53.610
PRs for documentation
and things like that.

00:40:53.610 --> 00:40:56.750
So that's a first step
that's accessible to anyone.

00:40:56.750 --> 00:41:02.150
But I think that a
really good repo-- and I

00:41:02.150 --> 00:41:04.040
think some of the
tidyverse repos do this.

00:41:04.040 --> 00:41:07.370
They will actually tag
their GitHub issues

00:41:07.370 --> 00:41:11.880
with good first issue as a tag,
meaning if you're just got off

00:41:11.880 --> 00:41:13.880
the bus, and you don't
know this project at all,

00:41:13.880 --> 00:41:17.240
you don't know the code, this
could be a good first issue

00:41:17.240 --> 00:41:19.640
for you to try to tackle.

00:41:19.640 --> 00:41:23.240
And you can even go to
a repo and say, hey,

00:41:23.240 --> 00:41:26.180
I see you guys don't have
any good first-issue tags.

00:41:26.180 --> 00:41:27.110
Can you make some?

00:41:27.110 --> 00:41:28.800
Because I'd like to
work on some issues.

00:41:28.800 --> 00:41:30.680
I can tell you
that the people who

00:41:30.680 --> 00:41:33.140
maintain those repos would
be very excited about someone

00:41:33.140 --> 00:41:35.550
who says like, what are
some issues I can work on?

00:41:35.550 --> 00:41:37.640
So I think working on
issues is a great way

00:41:37.640 --> 00:41:40.460
to learn the tech,
start interacting

00:41:40.460 --> 00:41:43.370
with the development team.

00:41:43.370 --> 00:41:48.950
And really, the sky's the limit.

00:41:48.950 --> 00:41:52.790
And if you really get involved
and really make contributions

00:41:52.790 --> 00:41:55.220
and really interact, I
think you can do a lot.

00:41:59.410 --> 00:42:00.100
Thank you.

00:42:00.100 --> 00:42:04.870
I see lots of other anonymous
questions coming in here too.

00:42:04.870 --> 00:42:10.060
And one was, there are lots of
options for teaching coding.

00:42:10.060 --> 00:42:12.190
Have you thought about
developing something

00:42:12.190 --> 00:42:15.370
for the younger generation
to begin learning R, like K

00:42:15.370 --> 00:42:16.930
through 12?

00:42:16.930 --> 00:42:19.450
We haven't.

00:42:19.450 --> 00:42:24.128
I think that would be awfully
interesting if someone could

00:42:24.128 --> 00:42:25.420
figure out how to do that well.

00:42:25.420 --> 00:42:29.000
I mean, really,
what we've gone--

00:42:29.000 --> 00:42:29.800
I have kids.

00:42:29.800 --> 00:42:34.690
And I've seen them learn coding
with the various self-paced

00:42:34.690 --> 00:42:35.690
online things.

00:42:35.690 --> 00:42:39.850
One complaint I have would
be, you can learn some tricks.

00:42:39.850 --> 00:42:44.530
And you can learn how to write
a loop, how to set a variable.

00:42:44.530 --> 00:42:47.230
But you don't actually
learn how to do the work.

00:42:47.230 --> 00:42:49.360
Because when you really
sit down and do the work,

00:42:49.360 --> 00:42:50.590
you're really frustrated.

00:42:50.590 --> 00:42:53.140
You have to learn
how to do research.

00:42:53.140 --> 00:42:55.240
You have to learn how
to read error messages.

00:42:55.240 --> 00:42:56.950
There's all this
stuff that is not

00:42:56.950 --> 00:42:59.800
covered in these
sort of drilling

00:42:59.800 --> 00:43:01.660
on these tactical skills.

00:43:01.660 --> 00:43:05.320
So with RStudio Academy
we've gone the other way.

00:43:05.320 --> 00:43:08.938
It's like, how can we have a
really thorough experience,

00:43:08.938 --> 00:43:10.480
where people are
doing real projects,

00:43:10.480 --> 00:43:13.670
and they have mentors,
and it's like real work.

00:43:13.670 --> 00:43:18.700
So that's the kind of thing I'd
want to see for high schoolers.

00:43:18.700 --> 00:43:20.120
You're actually doing real work.

00:43:20.120 --> 00:43:23.350
You have a mentor, not
you're just like cargo

00:43:23.350 --> 00:43:26.890
culting some tutorials.

00:43:26.890 --> 00:43:28.420
And that's the easy--

00:43:28.420 --> 00:43:32.860
it's easy to do the, hey, look,
self-paced tutorial learning.

00:43:32.860 --> 00:43:36.520
But it doesn't actually
really, really get people

00:43:36.520 --> 00:43:37.660
where they need to be.

00:43:37.660 --> 00:43:41.080
Maybe some of the work
that we're doing in Academy

00:43:41.080 --> 00:43:45.220
can eventually turn
into, hey, if you

00:43:45.220 --> 00:43:48.040
want to teach a course in
high school on data science,

00:43:48.040 --> 00:43:49.210
here's materials.

00:43:49.210 --> 00:43:50.500
And here's an approach.

00:43:50.500 --> 00:43:52.900
And here's a training
thing you can go to.

00:43:52.900 --> 00:43:56.410
So maybe some of that will
lead in that direction.

00:44:00.170 --> 00:44:05.170
Do you see Quarto helping to
fill the gaps between business

00:44:05.170 --> 00:44:09.130
users and data scientists?

00:44:09.130 --> 00:44:13.940
Yeah, I think
there's two ways.

00:44:13.940 --> 00:44:18.610
So the focus that we have
on creating data products,

00:44:18.610 --> 00:44:22.120
and not just data products
that are static, but like Shiny

00:44:22.120 --> 00:44:25.840
applications or reports
that you can parameterize,

00:44:25.840 --> 00:44:27.970
reports that you can
schedule, that is

00:44:27.970 --> 00:44:32.440
a way to take the expertise,
knowledge, and skills of data

00:44:32.440 --> 00:44:35.710
scientists and project
them in a flexible way

00:44:35.710 --> 00:44:37.610
throughout the rest
of the organization.

00:44:37.610 --> 00:44:42.550
So that's one piece, these
flexible, powerful, customized

00:44:42.550 --> 00:44:45.340
bespoke data products.

00:44:45.340 --> 00:44:49.460
And the other way is
to, I was saying before,

00:44:49.460 --> 00:44:52.370
like through the
tidyverse, trying

00:44:52.370 --> 00:44:56.060
to make people, who are
actually capable of quite

00:44:56.060 --> 00:44:58.670
a bit of technical work, but
have never done programming,

00:44:58.670 --> 00:45:01.610
to get them into a capacity
where they're actually

00:45:01.610 --> 00:45:05.510
able to take a data set and do
some of their own exploration.

