00:50:19.100 --> 00:50:23.750
But one on Slido was, what is
your relationship with R core

00:50:23.750 --> 00:50:26.460
development team?

00:50:26.460 --> 00:50:30.560
So a bunch of us have
known a bunch of people

00:50:30.560 --> 00:50:34.160
on the R core development
team for quite a while.

00:50:34.160 --> 00:50:38.150
I think Hadley's known people
on the R core team for 20 years.

00:50:38.150 --> 00:50:42.740
I've known a bunch of folks
on the team for over 10 years.

00:50:42.740 --> 00:50:45.590
So we are colleagues
in that way.

00:50:45.590 --> 00:50:47.270
We know each other.

00:50:47.270 --> 00:50:49.580
Sometimes there's
technical collaboration.

00:50:49.580 --> 00:50:53.510
We don't have actually anyone
in the company who's on R core.

00:50:53.510 --> 00:50:57.680
But we have some folks
that do quite a bit,

00:50:57.680 --> 00:50:59.630
Lionel Henry comes
to mind, folks

00:50:59.630 --> 00:51:02.150
that do quite a bit of
communication with them,

00:51:02.150 --> 00:51:05.660
provide patches, work on
stuff collaborating with them.

00:51:05.660 --> 00:51:13.250
But we don't have-- that's
entirely its own entity.

00:51:13.250 --> 00:51:17.540
I would characterize our
relationship as collegial.

00:51:17.540 --> 00:51:19.130
We've got our stuff
we're working on.

00:51:19.130 --> 00:51:20.360
And they've got their
stuff they're working on.

00:51:20.360 --> 00:51:21.402
And there's intersection.

00:51:21.402 --> 00:51:23.690
And we try to work
together to make things

00:51:23.690 --> 00:51:27.470
work as well as they
can when things come up

00:51:27.470 --> 00:51:33.260
that need to be resolved or
run aground or sorted out.

00:51:33.260 --> 00:51:38.772
We had a few times, when we've
had to really work together

00:51:38.772 --> 00:51:39.980
on something to get it right.

00:51:39.980 --> 00:51:42.080
And we've had a good
experience doing that.

00:51:45.600 --> 00:51:47.790
There was a question
earlier that I had

00:51:47.790 --> 00:51:51.120
missed that was around VS code.

00:51:51.120 --> 00:51:54.030
And, Sam and Allen,
thank you for helping,

00:51:54.030 --> 00:51:55.300
bring it to my attention.

00:51:55.300 --> 00:51:57.750
It was, with many
useful new extensions,

00:51:57.750 --> 00:52:00.720
VS Code is an alternative
that a lot of R programmers

00:52:00.720 --> 00:52:01.800
are switching to.

00:52:01.800 --> 00:52:05.760
Where do you see R
pseudo IDE in five years?

00:52:05.760 --> 00:52:12.960
Well, I do think that VS
code is an incredibly powerful

00:52:12.960 --> 00:52:18.370
but opinionated text-editing
programmers tool.

00:52:18.370 --> 00:52:22.223
So I think if you're willing
to put the time into learning

00:52:22.223 --> 00:52:24.390
it and customizing it, and
learning how to drive it,

00:52:24.390 --> 00:52:26.730
getting all your
extensions dialed,

00:52:26.730 --> 00:52:30.720
it's really powerful
and really great.

00:52:30.720 --> 00:52:32.970
RStudio has a
different philosophy.

00:52:32.970 --> 00:52:35.383
RStudio has much more
of an opinionated,

00:52:35.383 --> 00:52:37.050
these are the tools
you're going to need

00:52:37.050 --> 00:52:41.340
to do data analysis, presented
in a unified, clear, hopefully,

00:52:41.340 --> 00:52:43.560
straightforward and simple way.

00:52:43.560 --> 00:52:46.260
So it's actually trying
to do something different

00:52:46.260 --> 00:52:47.190
than VS code is.

00:52:47.190 --> 00:52:50.040
VS code is trying to be a
maximally powerful extensible

00:52:50.040 --> 00:52:51.150
programmer's editor.

00:52:51.150 --> 00:52:54.420
So I think it's possible to
get with various extensions.

00:52:54.420 --> 00:52:58.020
And we have our Quarto
extension for VS Code.

00:52:58.020 --> 00:53:00.180
We're definitely doing a
lot of work with VS code

00:53:00.180 --> 00:53:03.300
for people who want that
environment of like,

00:53:03.300 --> 00:53:04.890
I'm controlling everything.

00:53:04.890 --> 00:53:08.040
And I'm composing my IDE out
of all these different parts.

00:53:08.040 --> 00:53:10.230
And I'm customizing
and configuring.

00:53:10.230 --> 00:53:10.800
Great.

00:53:10.800 --> 00:53:15.360
But I actually think that many,
many, many data scientists need

00:53:15.360 --> 00:53:17.280
something that's more
straightforward and

00:53:17.280 --> 00:53:17.880
streamlined.

00:53:17.880 --> 00:53:19.890
And that's kind of
where RStudio fits.

00:53:24.000 --> 00:53:26.580
TJ, I see your
