00:55:51.750 --> 00:55:55.520
you do have to promote yourself.

00:55:55.520 --> 00:55:57.170
Lots of people
need to understand

00:55:57.170 --> 00:55:59.550
the value of what you're doing.

00:55:59.550 --> 00:56:05.390
And I imagine that's
a big part of thriving

00:56:05.390 --> 00:56:08.510
as a data-science team
inside a bigger environment.

00:56:08.510 --> 00:56:13.640
I think probably finding
the people for whom

00:56:13.640 --> 00:56:17.660
you can add a lot of
value and initiating work

00:56:17.660 --> 00:56:19.580
with them is a
good way to build.

00:56:19.580 --> 00:56:22.575
You want to have stakeholders
that are outside of your group

00:56:22.575 --> 00:56:24.950
really excited about what
you're doing and trying to help

00:56:24.950 --> 00:56:27.050
you grow what you're doing.

00:56:27.050 --> 00:56:32.120
So some of the times, you
have to go find those people

00:56:32.120 --> 00:56:35.040
in the organization.

00:56:35.040 --> 00:56:36.510
Definitely.

00:56:36.510 --> 00:56:37.780
Thank you.

00:56:37.780 --> 00:56:39.780
Another question over on Slido.

00:56:39.780 --> 00:56:43.320
And then any questions that
I have missed from anybody

00:56:43.320 --> 00:56:44.880
as we get to the
top of the hour,

00:56:44.880 --> 00:56:48.300
feel free to raise your hand
on Zoom here as well, or just

00:56:48.300 --> 00:56:50.950
re-copy it into the chat.

00:56:50.950 --> 00:56:54.060
But one was, what would
be the personality profile

00:56:54.060 --> 00:56:56.865
of an ideal Posit tech employee?

00:56:59.820 --> 00:57:00.780
It's interesting.

00:57:00.780 --> 00:57:03.090
I don't know.

00:57:03.090 --> 00:57:07.080
I would say, one of the
most important attributes

00:57:07.080 --> 00:57:11.430
would be, I would say,
humility about what

00:57:11.430 --> 00:57:13.260
you know and don't know.

00:57:13.260 --> 00:57:19.020
So even if you are really
smart and knowledgeable

00:57:19.020 --> 00:57:23.250
and capable to not walk into
situations, where you're

00:57:23.250 --> 00:57:24.960
collaborating, where
you bring that,

00:57:24.960 --> 00:57:30.630
to have the amateur's
mindset of like,

00:57:30.630 --> 00:57:32.220
I don't know what the answer is.

00:57:32.220 --> 00:57:34.630
Let's see if we can
figure it out together.

00:57:34.630 --> 00:57:37.555
I think that's the vibe of
technical collaboration here.

00:57:37.555 --> 00:57:39.930
And that's not always the vibe
of technical collaboration

00:57:39.930 --> 00:57:41.070
in companies.

00:57:41.070 --> 00:57:42.750
Sometimes, it's like
strong personality

00:57:42.750 --> 00:57:44.430
versus strong personality.

00:57:44.430 --> 00:57:46.840
And I would say we very
much don't have that.

00:57:46.840 --> 00:57:48.960
So that's one attribute.

00:57:48.960 --> 00:57:51.510
But beyond that, I
mean, we have people

00:57:51.510 --> 00:57:55.020
who love to go give
talks and be on Twitter.

00:57:55.020 --> 00:57:57.792
And we have people
who just don't ever

00:57:57.792 --> 00:58:00.000
want to have anyone talk to
them about anything ever.

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:04.290
You know, so there's a huge
variety of personality types

00:58:04.290 --> 00:58:06.600
for folks that work here.

00:58:06.600 --> 00:58:10.050
And we try to find a
place for all of them.

00:58:15.260 --> 00:58:16.200
Thank you very much.

00:58:16.200 --> 00:58:18.620
I don't see any other
questions that were

00:58:18.620 --> 00:58:20.130
coming to mind that I missed.

00:58:20.130 --> 00:58:23.300
So I'm going to go back
over to some of the Slido

00:58:23.300 --> 00:58:25.460
ones for the last minute here.

00:58:25.460 --> 00:58:29.720
But I also want to
remind everybody,

00:58:29.720 --> 00:58:33.890
because we were talking about
the topic of self promotion.

00:58:33.890 --> 00:58:36.290
In the beginning
when I was asking

00:58:36.290 --> 00:58:38.660
if you do have any feedback
about the Data Science

00:58:38.660 --> 00:58:41.990
Hangout and things that
you'd like to see next year,

00:58:41.990 --> 00:58:43.910
I wanted to make
sure I don't forget

00:58:43.910 --> 00:58:46.340
to share the survey with you.

00:58:46.340 --> 00:58:50.870
So let me just copy
that over there too.

00:58:50.870 --> 00:58:53.400
Thank you.

00:58:53.400 --> 00:58:58.480
Another question over here on--

00:58:58.480 --> 00:59:00.100
this is not a question,
but a comment.

00:59:00.100 --> 00:59:03.820
So thank you, J.J., for leading
Posit and helping me avoid

00:59:03.820 --> 00:59:07.998
SPSS years ago in grad school.

00:59:07.998 --> 00:59:08.498
Good.

00:59:12.210 --> 00:59:13.207
Oh, I see Andy.

00:59:13.207 --> 00:59:14.290
You just asked a question.

00:59:14.290 --> 00:59:15.415
I'll let you jump in first.

00:59:17.890 --> 00:59:18.670
Hi.

00:59:18.670 --> 00:59:21.950
So Posit is a B-Corporation,
a benefit corporation.

00:59:21.950 --> 00:59:25.060
And I'm wondering if you
have anything to share, aside

00:59:25.060 --> 00:59:28.600
from public benefit of bringing
this open-source tool to all

00:59:28.600 --> 00:59:32.260
of us that use it
for free, anything

00:59:32.260 --> 00:59:38.230
else around environmental or
the benefit corp structure?

00:59:38.230 --> 00:59:42.610
I mean, the biggest
differentiating characteristic

00:59:42.610 --> 00:59:45.700
of public benefit
corporations is that they are,

00:59:45.700 --> 00:59:49.210
unlike traditional C Corps--

00:59:49.210 --> 00:59:53.770
the evolution of C Corps
in the United States

00:59:53.770 --> 00:59:57.850
and the case law and
things, have made C Corps--

00:59:57.850 --> 01:00:01.090
they're pretty much exclusively
beholden to their shareholders

01:00:01.090 --> 01:00:03.340
as the stakeholder that matters.

01:00:03.340 --> 01:00:04.780
The biggest thing
about B Corp is

01:00:04.780 --> 01:00:09.730
it changes the corporate
law and corporate charter,

01:00:09.730 --> 01:00:13.870
so that your stakeholders
are considered more broadly,

01:00:13.870 --> 01:00:17.420
your customers, your
employees, your community,

01:00:17.420 --> 01:00:20.470
the environment, your
shareholders, all stakeholders.

01:00:20.470 --> 01:00:24.010
And your duty is
to run the company

01:00:24.010 --> 01:00:25.510
for the benefit of
all stakeholders

01:00:25.510 --> 01:00:28.820
and to balance their
concerns and interests.

01:00:28.820 --> 01:00:31.610
So that's the main thing.

01:00:31.610 --> 01:00:36.160
And then, the way the
law works is you also

01:00:36.160 --> 01:00:37.988
establish a public
benefit that you're

01:00:37.988 --> 01:00:39.280
accountable to working towards.

01:00:39.280 --> 01:00:41.860
In our case, it's
open-source software.

01:00:41.860 --> 01:00:49.730
We actually get rated by
the B-Lab every three years.

01:00:49.730 --> 01:00:51.400
And if you go look
at our report,

01:00:51.400 --> 01:00:53.930
there's quite a bit in
there about sustainability,

01:00:53.930 --> 01:00:55.210
environmental sustainability.

01:00:55.210 --> 01:01:00.143
And a bunch of the
points come from that.

01:01:00.143 --> 01:01:01.810
And we're actually
about to get rerated.

01:01:01.810 --> 01:01:06.580
So there'll be a new report
coming out next spring,

01:01:06.580 --> 01:01:09.670
hopefully by February or
March, that actually goes

01:01:09.670 --> 01:01:10.850
into more depth about that.

01:01:10.850 --> 01:01:12.820
But if you look at our
current benefit year report,

01:01:12.820 --> 01:01:15.403
you'll see the different things
that we've done and are doing.

01:01:19.060 --> 01:01:21.760
J.J., do you have a meeting
you're going to go run

01:01:21.760 --> 01:01:23.658
to in one minute here?

01:01:23.658 --> 01:01:24.950
Or do you have one more minute?

01:01:24.950 --> 01:01:27.988
Actually-- no, it's fine.

01:01:27.988 --> 01:01:30.530
There is another meeting going
on that I'm supposed to be at.

01:01:30.530 --> 01:01:34.400
But if we run over a
minute or two, that's fine.

01:01:34.400 --> 01:01:35.020
OK.

01:01:35.020 --> 01:01:35.770
Thank you.

01:01:35.770 --> 01:01:40.090
One question that
I did miss earlier

01:01:40.090 --> 01:01:42.310
was, what areas
do you anticipate

01:01:42.310 --> 01:01:47.110
Posit growing and any
career opportunities

01:01:47.110 --> 01:01:48.610
opposite in these areas?

01:01:51.760 --> 01:01:56.830
Well, one area of
significant growth that

01:01:56.830 --> 01:01:59.510
has occurred over the last
years is, like I said,

01:01:59.510 --> 01:02:02.373
we've been firing up
the Posit Academy.

01:02:02.373 --> 01:02:03.790
And we've hired
quite a few people

01:02:03.790 --> 01:02:06.650
as educators in that group.

01:02:06.650 --> 01:02:08.620
So if that continues
to go well, I

01:02:08.620 --> 01:02:11.180
think we'll definitely
be growing there.

01:02:11.180 --> 01:02:14.710
And then, I would say
that the Posit business

01:02:14.710 --> 01:02:19.330
model and balance between open
source and commercial software,

01:02:19.330 --> 01:02:21.710
I think we've sorted
out pretty well.

01:02:21.710 --> 01:02:25.030
And so I think that the
growth of the company

01:02:25.030 --> 01:02:29.150
will look approximately
like it is now.

01:02:29.150 --> 01:02:33.940
So we have I think
something like 42% or 43%

01:02:33.940 --> 01:02:36.020
of our engineers work
on open-source software.

01:02:36.020 --> 01:02:38.570
And then a bunch of others
work on commercial software.

01:02:38.570 --> 01:02:40.570
So in software
development, there's

01:02:40.570 --> 01:02:42.700
opportunities that are
both in open source.

01:02:42.700 --> 01:02:45.400
And the best way to, by the
way, get hired into open source

01:02:45.400 --> 01:02:47.502
is to be an open-source
contributor.

01:02:47.502 --> 01:02:49.960
I would say, there's almost no
one in our open-source group

01:02:49.960 --> 01:02:53.040
that just like applied as
like, I'm a software engineer.

01:02:53.040 --> 01:02:54.540
I'd like to do open
source at Posit.

01:02:54.540 --> 01:02:57.490
It was all people that
literally, were actually

01:02:57.490 --> 01:02:59.800
already doing
open-source software

01:02:59.800 --> 01:03:03.900
and actually collaborating
with us on GitHub, et cetera.

01:03:03.900 --> 01:03:05.650
And then we said, oh,
yeah, it'd be great.

01:03:05.650 --> 01:03:07.060
We should work together.

01:03:07.060 --> 01:03:09.670
So that's that.

01:03:09.670 --> 01:03:15.550
And then lots of opportunities
in sales and marketing

01:03:15.550 --> 01:03:20.320
and things like that that
you'll see on our website.

01:03:24.220 --> 01:03:25.060
Thank you, J.J..

01:03:25.060 --> 01:03:27.340
I'm seeing a lot of
comments in here.

01:03:27.340 --> 01:03:28.900
And this says,
J.J. For president,

01:03:28.900 --> 01:03:32.410
so something to
think about here.

01:03:32.410 --> 01:03:33.820
One last question.

01:03:33.820 --> 01:03:36.730
Thank you, Jonathan, for
the great question here.

01:03:36.730 --> 01:03:38.590
What are some
failures that you have

01:03:38.590 --> 01:03:39.970
learned from in your career?

01:03:39.970 --> 01:03:42.430
And how have you grown
from your experience?

01:03:45.580 --> 01:03:54.430
I'd say one failure was the
second company that I started.

01:03:54.430 --> 01:03:56.810
The first company I
started went pretty well.

01:03:56.810 --> 01:04:02.260
And I thought that, oh, the
skill that I was applying there

01:04:02.260 --> 01:04:02.900
was like--

01:04:08.245 --> 01:04:09.490
how do I want to say this?

01:04:12.170 --> 01:04:15.860
Coming up with brand-new ways
to work and brand-new ideas

01:04:15.860 --> 01:04:16.970
is a skill.

01:04:16.970 --> 01:04:19.490
And then that's what
startup founders do.

01:04:19.490 --> 01:04:22.067
And I, in my second
company, tried

01:04:22.067 --> 01:04:23.900
to create a whole bunch
of brand-new things,

01:04:23.900 --> 01:04:25.820
like, oh, re-imagine
your work this way.

01:04:25.820 --> 01:04:28.790
Do this totally different
thing that you're doing now.

01:04:28.790 --> 01:04:30.630
And that didn't go very well.

01:04:30.630 --> 01:04:34.730
And I took from that,
it's much better

01:04:34.730 --> 01:04:40.430
to be rooted in what you observe
people exactly what they're

01:04:40.430 --> 01:04:42.270
doing and try to
make that better.

01:04:42.270 --> 01:04:46.070
So as an example, when
I started working in R,

01:04:46.070 --> 01:04:47.540
I wasn't a data scientist.

01:04:47.540 --> 01:04:49.100
I wasn't an R programmer.

01:04:49.100 --> 01:04:51.130
And we easily could
have said, OK, we're

01:04:51.130 --> 01:04:53.630
going to reimagine how you work
with R. We're going to build

01:04:53.630 --> 01:04:54.770
this brand new interface.

01:04:54.770 --> 01:04:56.180
Forget about the console.

01:04:56.180 --> 01:04:57.240
Forget about it.

01:04:57.240 --> 01:04:59.240
But I said, well,
there's lots of people

01:04:59.240 --> 01:05:02.160
using R just this way.

01:05:02.160 --> 01:05:03.950
And so if I can just
make that better,

01:05:03.950 --> 01:05:06.150
then that's a good foundation.

01:05:06.150 --> 01:05:09.560
So I think overreaching
in product design

01:05:09.560 --> 01:05:13.400
is something that I definitely
paid for in the second company

01:05:13.400 --> 01:05:14.030
that I started.

01:05:14.030 --> 01:05:15.650
And I've tried to not do that.

01:05:15.650 --> 01:05:19.310
Try to stay close to
what people are doing.

01:05:28.830 --> 01:05:30.970
I think I alluded
to this before,

01:05:30.970 --> 01:05:35.700
but not knowing yourself
well and finding yourself

01:05:35.700 --> 01:05:40.050
with responsibilities
and a work situation

01:05:40.050 --> 01:05:44.790
where you're not being
nourished the way you need to,

01:05:44.790 --> 01:05:46.095
you can end up in that spot.

01:05:46.095 --> 01:05:47.970
And it's very hard to
dig yourself out of it.

01:05:51.670 --> 01:05:53.470
That's happened to me.

01:05:53.470 --> 01:05:54.130
Thank you.

01:05:54.130 --> 01:05:56.470
I know I said that
was the last question.

01:05:56.470 --> 01:05:58.240
But I don't want
to end on failure.

01:06:01.780 --> 01:06:04.720
I know something we
talk about at Posit

01:06:04.720 --> 01:06:09.460
is creating a company
that's around in 100 years.

01:06:09.460 --> 01:06:13.950
What do you think is one of the
most important lessons learned?

01:06:13.950 --> 01:06:16.080
Or things that you're
thinking about to get us

01:06:16.080 --> 01:06:18.580
to 100 years from now.

01:06:18.580 --> 01:06:20.610
Well, I think the
number one thing

01:06:20.610 --> 01:06:23.340
was get the right
corporate structure,

01:06:23.340 --> 01:06:24.900
so all the incentives
are aligned,

01:06:24.900 --> 01:06:28.170
so that's the only thing
we need to worry about.

01:06:28.170 --> 01:06:29.820
And we're not worried
about maximizing

01:06:29.820 --> 01:06:31.680
some short-term objective.

01:06:31.680 --> 01:06:34.140
So that's one.

01:06:34.140 --> 01:06:40.650
Now, two is going to
be leadership succession.

01:06:40.650 --> 01:06:42.870
So it's easy to
get caught up in,

01:06:42.870 --> 01:06:45.420
oh, hey, there's four
or five of us that

01:06:45.420 --> 01:06:46.740
are kind of leading things.

01:06:46.740 --> 01:06:48.090
And we're making it all work.

01:06:48.090 --> 01:06:51.510
And then, but we're
all going to get old.

01:06:51.510 --> 01:06:56.610
And so trying to figure out over
time who are the people that

01:06:56.610 --> 01:06:58.980
are going to succeed you and
giving them opportunities

01:06:58.980 --> 01:07:03.330
to even accelerate their
learning and bring them

01:07:03.330 --> 01:07:05.778
into the space
that you're in is,

01:07:05.778 --> 01:07:07.320
I think, where a
lot of organizations

01:07:07.320 --> 01:07:09.090
fail to be sustainable.

01:07:09.090 --> 01:07:11.130
They may be like
economically sustainable,

01:07:11.130 --> 01:07:13.005
they may have the right
corporate incentives.

01:07:13.005 --> 01:07:15.400
But then succession
does them in.

01:07:15.400 --> 01:07:18.090
So it's not like
right on our doorstep,

01:07:18.090 --> 01:07:20.850
but it's something that's
on my mind to think about.

01:07:23.570 --> 01:07:24.530
Thank you.

01:07:24.530 --> 01:07:28.558
Thank you so much, J.J., for
joining us today and sharing

01:07:28.558 --> 01:07:29.225
your experience.

01:07:29.225 --> 01:07:29.808
My pleasure.

01:07:29.808 --> 01:07:32.190
This has been really fun.

01:07:32.190 --> 01:07:35.750
And thank you all for being
a part of the Data Science

01:07:35.750 --> 01:07:36.680
Hangout today.

